-The knee redesigns are graded one joint at a time against a single target curve, and the capability survey extends the grading to two joint coordinates at a time across the leg's muscle actuators. For each muscle actuator, torque and active-force capability were graded on a $5 \times 5$ grid over the two joint coordinates its routing crosses, with the hip adduction coordinate bounded by a linear envelope coupled to hip flexion. The human requirement is the torque surface of the same-named single muscle of the stock Gait2392 model on its native routing (OpenSim 4.4.1), the product of OpenSim moment arm and maximum isometric force \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2018}. The robot side applies the characterized Festo force model \citep{bolen_2026} at \qty{620}{\kPa}, with the same BPA sizes on both routings. A grid cell counts toward the torque capability where the BPA torque carries the human torque's sign and its magnitude meets or exceeds the human magnitude, and toward the active-force capability where the actuator is stretched past rest and holds taut torque; the reported quantities are the shares of the graded domain's area on which each condition holds.
-
-The survey compares two routings. The hand-tuned routing is the original master's-project layout of the robot-body model \citep{morrow_optimization_2020}. The optimized routing re-solves each muscle under the transmission freedoms of Section~\ref{sec:route_redesign}: BPAs are added in parallel, up to three per muscle, and the tendon is reeved over a tackle of up to three line parts per joint crossed, with the actuator sizes re-solved per configuration and the configuration chosen by a bounded per-muscle search. Nineteen of the 27 actuators carry complete graded records on both routings, and the survey is reported over those 19.
-
-Table~\ref{tab:CapabilitySurvey} gives the aggregate. The hand-tuned routing meets or exceeds the human torque over \qty{66.32}{\percent} of the graded domain and delivers active force over \qty{97.05}{\percent}, and the optimized routing covers \qty{75.30}{\percent} of the graded domain on torque and \qty{95.23}{\percent} on active force. Per actuator, the optimized routing meets or exceeds the human torque over a strictly larger share on 11 of the 19 actuators, with 3 ties and 5 actuators lower, and 5 of the 19 improve on both measures. The active-force share was already near-complete for the hand-tuned routing on most actuators, so the improvement the optimized routing buys lies almost entirely in torque coverage.
+\subsection{Whole-Leg Capability Against Human Torque Surfaces}\label{sec:capability_survey}
+
+The capability survey grades each actuator against the same-named single muscle of the Gait2392 model \citep{delp_opensim_2007,seth_opensim_2018} on a $5\times5$ grid of two joint coordinates. The hip adduction domain is clipped by its flexion-dependent envelope. A cell meets the requirement when the BPA is taut and its torque has the human torque's sign and at least its magnitude. The coverage metric is the fraction of included grid cells meeting that condition; the aggregate is the unweighted mean of the per-actuator fractions, not a continuous-area integral or a whole-leg torque sum.
+
+The BPA force model is evaluated at \qty{620}{\kPa} \citep{bolen_2026}. The comparison includes all 27 actuator sites (Supplementary Table~\ref{tab:survey_dofs_sizes}). The baseline uses the master's-era attachment routing at unity transmission; its eight nominal \qty{40}{\mm} sites are evaluated with four parallel \qty{20}{\mm} BPAs, while the other sites use the survey's base configuration. The updated side selects the saved transmission-ladder winner at each site, with actuator length and tendon length re-solved per configuration. It retains the same underlying attachment routing and the original diameter specification, including \qty{40}{\mm} actuators at those eight sites. The comparison therefore combines transmission, actuator sizing, and the baseline diameter substitution; it does not isolate an effect of attachment placement or compare two all-\qty{20}{\mm} assemblies.
+
+The mean grid coverage increases from \qty{37.28}{\percent} to \qty{65.19}{\percent}, improving at 23 sites and tying at four (Table~\ref{tab:CapabilitySurvey}). A separate sign-aware torque-ratio score averages $\min(M_R/M_H,1)$ over the graded cells, assigning zero to inactive cells. Its mean falls from $-1.93$ to $-3.63$. Negative ratios penalize torque in the wrong direction, so greater coverage can coexist with a worse mean ratio where severe wrong-sign predictions remain. These metrics support different conclusions and should be considered together.
+
+For comparison, the average of the two axis-wise mean coverages is \qty{38.39}{\percent} for the baseline and \qty{66.17}{\percent} for the updated configurations. On a complete rectangular grid, averaging row and column coverages equals the overall cell fraction exactly; it cannot reduce the influence of edge poses there. The two summaries differ only on the four hip-envelope domains with excluded cells. The two metrics and the torque-ratio score are reported together, so their different weightings remain visible.
 
